  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Troughing progressing over the eastern \nGreat Lakes and southeast Canada will continue to slowly trek east \nthis afternoon through Friday. The associated ~1008 mb surface low \npressure centered over western Quebec will also weaken and follow \nsuit to the east through this time. Meanwhile, ridging will amplify \nacross the middle of the country late this week and hold through the \nholiday weekend, building surface high pressure across Hudson \nBay/Ontario that will nose down into the Great Lakes by Saturday/ \nSunday. Ridging may finally begin to fold overtop of the Great Lakes \nregion heading into the middle of next week as troughing works \nacross the west from the Pacific coast.\n\nForecast Details: Showers/storms continue to percolate across \nsouthern reaches of the forecast area into mid-Michigan early \nthis afternoon. Not a ton of change to this through early \nevening with the highest PoPs focused near and south of M-72. \nWhile severe weather doesn't appear to be a glaring threat, \nsuppose there's enough support there for a low chance of a \nstronger storm capable of producing gusty winds -- lining up \nwell with SPC's latest D1 outlook. Primary concern continues to \nbe locally heavy rainfall. Areas that received 1-3.5\"+ of rain \nover the last several hours (far southwestern portions of the \nCWA) will be most susceptible to potential localized flooding \nconcerns with any additional locally heavy downpours.\n\nExpecting a break in shower/storm coverage mid-late evening, but \nlatest trends showing increased support for additional upstream \nshowers/storms to make inroads across parts of northern Michigan \ntonight into Friday morning. While lots of uncertainty riddles this \ntime frame (partially hinging on how this afternoon/evening shake \nout across the broader region), think that if precip is able to \nsurvive into northern Michigan later tonight that'll it'll be \nprimary a locally heavy rain threat rather than a severe weather \nthreat. Probabilities for 1\"+ of rain have increased over the last \nseveral forecast cycles with even some low probabilities for 2\"+ \nnoted (these higher end chances maximized south of APX's CWA). \n\nWhile some shower/storm chances will linger Friday into Friday \nevening, a general drying trend is anticipated heading into the \nholiday weekend. Mostly sunny skies and largely precip-free \nconditions anticipated Saturday - Monday with daily high \ntemperatures near to several degrees above early September normals. \nChances for a return to more active conditions look to hold off \nuntil the Tuesday - Wednesday time frame next week.",
